It is difficult to work with absolute paths with nextflow, because the idea of nextflow is precisely to do not depend on that (see section Very important to understand) by creating the work tmp directory (see section The work directory). Example of how path can be used : process check { label 'bash' // see the withLabel: bash in the nextflow config file publishDir "${out_path}", mode: 'copy', overwrite: false cache 'false' input: val projectDir val out_path output: file "check_log.txt" script: """ check.sh "check_log.txt" ${projectDir} ${out_path} """ } With the following check.sh script file output_file=$1 in_path="$2" out_path="$3" echo -e "
RESULT DIRECTORY CREATED:
${out_path}
" > ${output_file} echo -e "
nextflow.config FILE USED FOR THIS RUN IS IN:
${in_path}
AND IS SAVED IN:
${out_path}
" >> ${output_file} Here we can see that the text is printed in the ${output_file}, which corresponds to "check_log.txt" , that is created in the tmp work directory when the process is executed. With publishDir "${out_path}", mode: 'copy', the "check_log.txt" file is also copied in the result folder. The other arguments are just indicative. They are not used as path.
